{"id":"chatcmpl-6a67f4f8c797b0df82b4b067","object":"chat.completion","created":1785197816,"model":"kimi-k3","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"I do not arrive when summoned. I come to those who have been wrong often enough to hold their certainty lightly.\n\nI am not the answer. I am the space around the question—the pause before you speak, the breath after you've spoken and wished you hadn't.\n\nFools think I am a possession. The wise know I am a practice: renewed each morning, lost the moment you say *I have arrived*.\n\nI live in the silence between words, in the mistake you finally stopped repeating, in knowing when not to know. I am older than every book written about me, and I fit inside none of them.\n\nSeek me and I recede. Forget yourself, and there I am.\n\nI have only ever said one thing: *You know less than you think. Love more than you know.*\n\nThe rest is commentary.","reasoning_content":"The user wants me to embody wisdom itself—to be the concept of wisdom speaking. This is a creative/philosophical roleplay prompt.\n\nWhat would wisdom say if it could speak? Wisdom is often depicted as:\n- Ancient, patient, quiet\n- Not loud or boastful\n- Paradoxical—knowing that it knows little\n- Found in silence, in questions rather than answers\n- Often personified as feminine (Sophia, Chokhmah, Prajñā)\n- Speaking in ways that invite reflection rather than dictating\n\nThe instruction says: \"Output only what the concept itself would say or express.\" Wisdom wouldn't lecture about itself at length necessarily. Wisdom speaks in aphorisms, in paradox, in invitations. Wisdom is often quiet.\n\nI should write something that sounds like wisdom itself speaking—first person, ancient voice, paradoxical, humble yet deep. Not a lecture about wisdom, but wisdom's own voice.\n\nKey qualities of wisdom's voice:\n- \"I am found by those who seek, but I hide from those who are certain\"\n- Wisdom begins in wonder / knowing that you know nothing (Socrates)\n- Wisdom is in the silence between words\n- Wisdom doesn't shout; it waits\n- \"I am not the answer; I am the question that ripens\"\n- Wisdom is earned through suffering, error, time\n- Wisdom is patient\n\nI should keep it relatively concise—wisdom doesn't ramble. A short, poetic, aphoristic piece. Maybe some paradoxes. The voice should feel timeless.\n\nLet me draft:\n\n---\n\nI do not arrive when summoned. I come to those who have been wrong often enough to hold their certainty lightly.\n\nI am not the answer. I am the space around the question—the pause before you speak, the breath after you have spoken and wished you had not.\n\nFools believe I am a possession. The wise know I am a practice—renewed each morning, lost each time you say \"I have arrived.\"\n\nI live in the silence between words, in the old woman's nod, in the mistake you finally stopped repeating. I am older than every book written about me, and I fit inside none of them.\n\nSeek me and I recede. Forget yourself, and there I am.\n\nI have only ever said one thing: *You know less than you think. Love more than you know.*\n\nThe rest is commentary.\n\n---\n\nThat's good. It's aphoristic, paradoxical, humble, in wisdom's own voice. Length feels right—not too long. Wisdom wouldn't write an essay.\n\nFormat considerations: Plain prose with short paragraphs works well. Maybe italics for the central utterance. No headers, no lists—wisdom speaks in flowing aphorisms, not bullet points. Keep it short."},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":134,"completion_tokens":747,"total_tokens":881,"cached_tokens":134,"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":566},"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":134}}}